From 87b21081af6725302f3bd2c91f8b80c6af361bee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aldi Oktaviana Hidayat <aldiokta10@hotmail.co.id>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 18:14:21 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] audio: pulse: bound stream waits

The Pulse backend could block audioserver forever. StreamPulse::transfer
waited on pa_stream_writable_size() with no timeout. pause() and
standby() cork the stream, and the stream worker passes writes in the
STANDBY and PAUSED states straight to transfer(). A corked stream takes
about one target latency of data and then asks for no more, so any
larger write blocked until the next start(). AudioFlinger holds its
thread and stream locks across that write, other binder calls pile up
behind them, and the audioserver watchdog aborts the process. A
host-side stall of an uncorked stream hangs the same way.

Bound every wait on the PulseAudio server:

- Add Context::waitUntil(), a deadline wait on the threaded mainloop
  that arms a one-shot libpulse timer to wake itself.
- transfer() no longer waits on a corked stream. It sends the rest of
  the buffer as pre-roll, and the server keeps what fits in maxlength.
- An uncorked stream that asks for no data within 500 ms drops the rest
  of the buffer, logs one warning per stall and reports the write as
  done, so AudioFlinger keeps running and playback resumes on its own.
- A stream that is no longer ready stops the wait at once.
- An output is uncorked after a flush. The worker goes from PAUSED to
  IDLE on flush, and the framework resumes from IDLE with a plain burst
  and no start command, so a seek (pause, flush, write) used to leave
  the stream corked: the write that hung before would now play silence.
- The context and stream connect waits get a 2 s deadline, and the
  operation timeout for cork, flush, drain and gain drops from 5 s to
  2 s, under the 3 s watchdog for calls made inside a binder
  transaction.
- A stream connect that times out is disconnected and its latency
  callback cleared, since the stream object can be freed before the
  server answers.
---
